    explanation: `**Wrestling**, in an MMA context, is the set of skills built around taking an opponent to the ground (or preventing them from being taken down), and around controlling position once there. It draws heavily on amateur wrestling styles, chiefly freestyle and folkstyle, adapted for a sport that also allows striking, submissions and a cage or ring wall to wrestle against.

A fighter with a strong wrestling background is often able to dictate where a fight is fought: taking it to the ground when that suits them, or using the same skills in reverse to keep a fight standing by stopping an opponent's own takedown attempts.`,
    howItWorks: `Wrestling in MMA covers three broad jobs: getting a fight to the ground (takedowns), keeping it there once it arrives (control and mat return), and stopping the same thing from happening to you (takedown defence, including the sprawl). Unlike a pure wrestling match, an MMA wrestling exchange also has to account for strikes landing during the attempt, and for a cage or ring wall a fighter can use to their advantage rather than simply being pushed out of bounds.`,
